2. Parallel gripper: SO-ARM-102 by Robonine (CERN-OHL-P-2.0 / CC BY 4.0)

The simulated gripper's opening, stroke and frame size, and the "stock" finger
entrant, were measured from the open-source SO-ARM-102 parallel gripper: Robonine,
SO-ARM 102, https://github.com/roboninecom/SO-ARM-102, commit 190eb162. Its hardware
sources are licensed CERN-OHL-P-2.0 and its documentation and simulation files
CC BY 4.0. The stock entrant is our own box approximation of its finger, and the
printable jaw in the print pack is drawn from the documented interface dimensions
only. This repository contains no files from that project. If a later version
includes or modifies its CAD, that file will carry the CERN-OHL-P-2.0 notice and a
statement of the modifications, as the licence requires.


3. NVIDIA Nemotron models (used through an API)

Designs, hypotheses and agent turns are generated by NVIDIA Nemotron 3 models
(nvidia/nemotron-3-super-120b-a12b and nvidia/Nemotron-3_5-Lightning), served by
Nebius Token Factory. No model weights are included. Recorded model
outputs (response caches, session recordings) are part of the results. The models'
own licences and terms are on their model cards.


